Yes, it is done on the downsized data. In function g.getmeta raw data is aggregated in short and long epochs. The long epochs (default 15 minutes) are used for non-wear detection and calculation of the clipping score, while the short epochs are used for the rest of the analyses.
Lines 560-573 from function g.getmeta <http://g.getmeta> indicate that the threshold for the range is 50 for the non-commercial GENEA (used in the 2011 PLoSONE paper on energy expenditure in pregnant and non-pregnant women) and 150 for all other accelerometer brands.
As I mentioned in an earlier correspondence in this group this range threshold is probably redundant because the standard deviation threshold already makes that a wide range is almost impossible.
Vincent

